.TH CDYNMQ_CREATE_MP 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cdynmq_create_mp \- create a dynamic queue with no size limit and with custom memory management
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

cdynmq *cdynmq_create_mp(
                         ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mmgmt_procs, char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a queue with no size limit, kept in a doubly-linked list. A
send never waits for free space. It fails instead, when an allocation
fails, when the queue already holds ccol_max_elem_count messages, or
when sends were turned off with cdynmq_disable_sending(3). A new
queue starts with sends on.

Every allocation, at creation and for each list node that a later send
adds, goes through mmgmt_procs. The queue keeps its own copy of the
procedures and frees itself through them, so the caller's
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t doesn't have to outlive this call. NULL selects
the default malloc and free, which is what the
cdynmq_create() macro passes.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
mmgmt_procs  Custom memory management procedures, or NULL to use the
             default malloc and free. If the value is not NULL, all of
             malloc, calloc, realloc and free must be set.
err_str      Optional pointer that gets an error string on failure. Pass
             NULL to ignore it.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
On success, a pointer to the new dynamic queue, and *err_str is set to
NULL when err_str is not NULL.

On failure, NULL, and *err_str describes the reason when err_str is not
NULL. The call fails when mmgmt_procs is malformed because it sets some
but not all of malloc, calloc, realloc and free, when an allocation
fails, when the queue's mutex or its condition variable cannot be
initialized, and, in a build with fork-safety protection compiled in,
when the queue's mutex cannot be registered with the fork-safety
registry. That registration also fails once the library's process-exit
destructors have run and the last queue is gone, for example in an
application destructor that runs after them. None of these failures
leaves anything allocated.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
All operations on this queue are thread-safe. When you are done with
the queue, destroy it with cdynmq_destroy().

fork(2) and this queue can be combined in two supported ways: call
fork(2) before you create the queue, or create it and then call fork(2)
followed at once by exec(3). A program that keeps running with no
exec(3) after the fork while a queue from before the fork is live is
not supported. As a defense in depth beyond that rule, the child never
inherits a lock that another thread held, in the locked state, at the
instant of the fork. The queue owns no thread of its own either, so a
queue that a child inherits this way stays fully usable.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cdynmq_create (3),
.BR cdynmq_destroy (3),
.BR cdynmq_send (3),
.BR cdynmq_recv (3)
